WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.460
In the third season of "Yellowstone," viewers
finally learned the source of Beth's animosity

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:05.670
toward her brother Jamie.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:09.690
A flashback shows they were close as teenagers,
and Beth turned to her brother for help when

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.630
she discovered she was pregnant.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:16.470
Jamie's solution was to take her to the abortion clinic that serves the region's indigenous population.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.939
Jamie is informed that the procedure will
amount to sterilization for Beth, but he goes

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:22.859
ahead and consents on her behalf.

00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:25.260
He doesn't even tell Beth about the consequences.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.220
When she finally learns the truth, she can't
forgive him.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:33.070
Time notes that many Native American women
were sterilized, legally and without their

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.430
knowledge, at reservation abortion clinics
in the 1970s.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:40.230
But that practice ended well before Beth would
have been getting her abortion.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.030
It's also hard to believe that Jamie would
be allowed to consent for his sister since

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.620
he's not her parent or legal guardian.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.860
Plus, considering that the clinic staff seem
to recognize them as children of the powerful

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.160
Dutton family, it seems like they'd at least
mention this to Beth before going through

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:55.660
with it.

